<article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" dtd-version="1.3" xml:lang="en" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Craniovertebr Junction Spine</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">J Craniovertebr Junction Spine</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">JCVJS</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">J Craniovert Jun Spine</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Journal of Craniovertebral Junction &#x00026; Spine</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0974-8237</issn><issn pub-type="epub">0976-9285</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Wolters Kluwer - Medknow</publisher-name><publisher-loc>India</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">41377836</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12688297</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">JCVJS-16-458</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.4103/jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Original Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Are C1 screws needed in occipitocervical fusion for traumatic cervical spine injury?</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Zeoli</surname><given-names>Tyler</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Jain</surname><given-names>Harsh</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Oliviera</surname><given-names>Nick De</given-names></name><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ye</surname><given-names>Emma</given-names></name><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ahluwalia</surname><given-names>Ranbir</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Younus</surname><given-names>Iyan</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Abtahi</surname><given-names>Amir M.</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="aff3" ref-type="aff">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Stephens</surname><given-names>Byron F.</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="aff3" ref-type="aff">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Zuckerman</surname><given-names>Scott L.</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="aff3" ref-type="aff">3</xref><xref rid="cor1" ref-type="corresp"/></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Department of Neurological Surgery, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, USA</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>2</label>Vanderbilt University School of Medicine, Nashville, TN, USA</aff><aff id="aff3"><label>3</label>Department of Orthopedic Surgery, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, USA</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1"><bold>Address for correspondence:</bold> Dr. Scott L. Zuckerman, Department of Neurological Surgery, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Medical Center North T-4224, Nashville 37212, TN, USA. E-mail: <email xlink:href="scott.zuckerman@vumc.org">scott.zuckerman@vumc.org</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Oct-Dec</season><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>20</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>16</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>458</fpage><lpage>464</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>28</day><month>7</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>24</day><month>9</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright: &#x000a9; 2025 Journal of Craniovertebral Junction and Spine</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncsalicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-Non Commercial-No Derivatives License 4.0 (CCBY-NC-ND), where it is permissible to download and share the work provided it is properly cited. The work cannot be changed in any way or used commercially without permission from the journal.</license-p></license></permissions><abstract><sec><title>Introduction:</title><p>While C1 screws provide an additional fixation point in occipitocervical (OC) fusion, they are often skipped due to surgical feasibility. In patients undergoing OC fusion with atlantooccipital dissociation (AOD), we sought to evaluate the impact of skipping C1 screws on: (1) construct length, (2) perioperative outcomes, and (3) long-term outcomes.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods:</title><p>A retrospective cohort study was performed for patients with traumatic cervical injury with AOD requiring OC fusion from 2003 to 2022. The primary outcome was total levels of fusion. Perioperative outcomes included operative time, estimated blood loss, and postoperative infections. Long-term outcomes included mechanical complications and reoperation. Bivariate and multivariable linear regression controlling for age, sex, and body mass index was performed.</p></sec><sec><title>Results:</title><p>Ninety-two patients underwent OC fusion with AOD (mean age: 40.2 &#x000b1; 17.2 years) with a median follow-up of 0.9 (interquartile range: 0.4&#x02013;2.8) years, and 54 (58.7%) received C1 instrumentation. Instrumenting C1 led to decreased fusion levels (2.5 &#x000b1; 0.8 vs. 3.8 &#x000b1; 1.0, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001) but increased operative time (192.7 &#x000b1; 68.8 vs. 166.3 &#x000b1; 40.5 min, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.032), blood loss (369.8 &#x000b1; 424.8 vs. 167.0 &#x000b1; 95.8 ml, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.002), and postoperative infections (11.1% vs. 0.0%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.040). There was no difference in mechanical complications (1.9% vs. 2.6%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 1.000) or reoperation (5.6% vs. 7.9%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.688). Mechanical complications were screw loosening (50.0%), instrumentation failure (50.0%), and pseudarthrosis (50.0%). On multivariable linear regression, C1 instrumentation was independently associated with decreased levels fused (&#x003b2; = &#x02212;-1.06, 95% confidence interval = &#x02212;-1.56 &#x02013; -0.67, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001).</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusion:</title><p>In OC fusion for cervical trauma, 41% of patients did not receive C1 screws. Skipping C1 was associated with longer constructs but reduced operative time, blood loss, and infection, without affecting complication or reoperation rates, highlighting the trade-offs of skipping C1 fixation.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>Atlantooccipital dissociation</kwd><kwd>C1 screw</kwd><kwd>fusion length</kwd><kwd>infections</kwd><kwd>occipitocervical fusion</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><funding-statement>Nil.</funding-statement></funding-group></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="intro"><title>INTRODUCTION</title><p>The incidence of cervical spine fractures in the United States is approximately 30 cases per 100,000 person-years.[<xref rid="R1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref><xref rid="R2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>] Among these, injuries involving the occipitocervical (OC) junction are particularly concerning due to the risk of spinal instability and neurological deficits.[<xref rid="R3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref><xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref><xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>] In atlantooccipital dissociation (AOD) dissociation, there remains an inherent risk for increased morbidity and mortality, in addition to significant healthcare costs related to concomitant spinal cord injuries.[<xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>]</p><p>OC fusions restore stability to the AO joint in the setting of various pathologies, including craniocervical junction instability, odontoid fractures with AO dislocation, occipital condyle fractures with associated craniocervical malalignment, and destabilizing fractures of C1/C2.[<xref rid="R3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref><xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref><xref rid="R7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref><xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref><xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>] Placement of a C1 screw during OC fusion enhances construct strength by adding fixation, improving load distribution, and promoting fusion, which helps mitigate adjacent segment degeneration.[<xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref><xref rid="R11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref><xref rid="R12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref><xref rid="R13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref><xref rid="R14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>] Despite these advantages, C1 screw placement remains technically demanding due to anatomical constraints.[<xref rid="R12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>] The proximity of the vertebral artery and C2 nerve root poses a significant risk for neurovascular injury, particularly during exposure of the C1 lateral mass.[<xref rid="R7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref><xref rid="R15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref><xref rid="R16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>] Although C2 nerve root sacrifice improves visualization,[<xref rid="R17" ref-type="bibr">17</xref><xref rid="R18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref><xref rid="R19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref><xref rid="R20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref><xref rid="R21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>] some advocate preserving the nerve to avoid complications such as occipital neuralgia or alopecia. Anatomical variations, such as ponticulus posticus or high-riding vertebral arteries, limit space for safe C1 screw placement.[<xref rid="R22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>] While omitting the C1 screw simplifies surgery, its biomechanical consequences are not fully defined and may necessitate extending the fusion construct, potentially limiting motion.[<xref rid="R14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref><xref rid="R23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref><xref rid="R24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]</p><p>Given these considerations, the decision to place a C1 screw during OC fusion remains variable and often depends on surgeon preference, anatomical limitations, and intraoperative factors. In light of the varying practice in instrumenting C1 when performing an OC fusion, in patients undergoing OC fusion for traumatic cervical spine injury, we sought to evaluate the impact of skipping the C1 screw on: (1) construct length, (2) perioperative outcomes, and (3) long-term outcomes.</p><sec><title>METHODS</title></sec><sec><title>Study design</title><p>A single-institution, retrospective cohort study of patients with traumatic cervical injury requiring OC fusion from 2003 to 2022 was undertaken. This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board. Informed consent was not required because of the retrospective nature of the study.</p></sec><sec><title>Study population</title><p>Registry data were selected for patients undergoing OC fusion after a traumatic cervical spine injury between 2003 and 2022. Inclusion criteria were adult patients (&#x02265;18 years) with traumatic cervical spine injury who underwent OC fusion. All traumatic spine injuries consisted of significant disruption of the AO joints, consistent with AOD.[<xref rid="R25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>] Moreover, all patients with subaxial injuries were excluded. The decision to perform an OC fusion was left up to the decision of the attending spine surgeon. Follow-up time was extended to the last clinical follow-up.</p></sec><sec><title>Exposure variable</title><p>The primary exposure variable was the skipping of the C1 screw during OC fusion, defined as the omission of C1 lateral mass screws during the OC fusion. The study population was binarized into those with a C1 screw versus those without a C1 screw. Additional exposure variables included patient demographics and preoperative variables. The preoperative variables included age, sex, body mass index (BMI), race, smoking status, and any medical comorbidities.</p></sec><sec><title>Outcome variables</title><p>The primary outcome of interest was the total <italic toggle="yes">number of levels fused</italic>. For example, if the occiput, C2, C3, and C4 were instrumented, the total fused levels would be 4, consisting of O/C1, C1/2, C2/3, and C3/4. Even if C1 was skipped, that level was counted in the fusion.</p><p>Secondary outcomes included both <italic toggle="yes">perioperative outcomes</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">long-term outcomes</italic>. Perioperative outcomes included operative time, estimated blood loss, and postoperative surgical site infections (SSI), both deep and superficial. SSIs were defined as positive wound/blood cultures, a reoperation for deep SSI, or superficial infection requiring antibiotics. Long-term outcomes included mechanical complications, including screw loosening, instrumentation fracture, including rod fracture or occipital plate fracture, pseudarthroses, and reoperation.</p></sec><sec><title>Surgical treatment</title><p>Each OC fusion varied slightly by surgeon, but the general surgical description is described here. In the prone position, the head is secured in Mayfield pins in an adequate anatomic alignment and is confirmed via fluoroscopy. Starting at the inion, a midline incision is extended to the planned lower instrumented vertebrae (LIV). After dissecting the soft tissue and identifying the bony landmarks, a posterior C1/C2 laminectomy can be completed. The Penfield one is used to dissect laterally along the C1/2 arch, often encountering a venous plexus. The Penfield can be used to expose the C2 nerve root traversing the C1/2 foramen and may be sacrificed to assist in the visualization of the lateral mass. C1 may be instrumented at the lateral mass, whereas C2 screws can be placed in the pars, pedicle, or lamina. An occipital plate is utilized below the inion at the midline, with several screws inserted for fixation. In cases of AO dissociation, reduction of the subluxation can be attempted manually. The rods are placed, and allograft is utilized after confirming the reduction using fluoroscopy.[<xref rid="R26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]</p></sec><sec><title>Statistical analysis</title><p>Patients who received a C1 screw during OC fusion were compared to those who did not. Descriptive statistics were reported for the groups. While mean and standard deviation were used to describe the continuous variables, categorical variables were described as frequencies. The Shapiro&#x02013;Wilk test and <italic toggle="yes">F</italic> test were used to assess the normal distribution and variance for continuous variables, respectively. Parametrically distributed continuous data were compared using the two-tailed <italic toggle="yes">t</italic>-test, and Wilcoxon or Mann&#x02013;Whitney <italic toggle="yes">U</italic> test was used to compare nonparametric data. Chi-square or Fisher&#x02019;s exact test was used to compare nominal data. Multivariable linear regression controlling for age, sex, and BMI was performed. An <italic toggle="yes">&#x003b1;</italic> &#x0003c;0.05 was considered statistically significant. All analysis was performed using IBM SPSS v29 (Armonk, New York, USA).</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results"><title>RESULTS</title><sec><title>Patient demographics</title><p>A total of 92 patients underwent OC fusion following cervical trauma with AOD. The mean age was 40.2 &#x000b1; 17.2 years, and 58 (63.0%) were males. All patients included had AOD, occipital condyle fracture was seen in 4 (4.3%), C1 fracture in 6 (6.5%), and C2 fracture in 9 (9.8%). The median follow-up time was 0.9 (interquartile range: 0.4&#x02013;2.8) years. The C1 was instrumented in 54 (58.7%) patients. Patients with C1 instrumentation were younger (33.9 &#x000b1; 13.7 vs. 49.2 &#x000b1; 17.7 years, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001) than those with a skipped C1 screw. There was no other difference with respect to the demographics between the 2 groups [<xref rid="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-T1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>]. Importantly, the two groups had similar rates of C1 fractures (1.9 vs. 13.2%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.078) and C2 fractures (5.6% vs. 15.8%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.154), as these concomitant injuries would potentially necessitate a longer fusion.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Perioperative variables</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1"><thead valign="top"><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Characteristic</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Total (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=92)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">With C1 screw, (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=54), <italic toggle="yes">mean&#x000b1;SD</italic></th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Without C1 screw (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=38), <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> (%)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>
</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">40.2&#x000b1;17.2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">33.9&#x000b1;13.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">49.2&#x000b1;17.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sex, male</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58 (63.0)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">33 (61.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">25 (65.8)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.647</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BMI</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">29.6&#x000b1;7.4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">29.1&#x000b1;6.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">30.2&#x000b1;8.2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.470</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Race, white</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Diabetes</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13 (14.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (11.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7 (18.4)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.322</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Smoking status</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.088</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Current</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">27 (29.3)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20 (37.0)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7 (18.4)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Former</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">21 (22.8)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9 (16.7)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 (31.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Never</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">44 (47.8)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">25 (46.3)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">19 (50.0)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Operative time</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">181.2&#x000b1;59.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">192.7&#x000b1;68.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">166.3&#x000b1;40.5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.032</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Blood loss</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">284.2&#x000b1;342.6</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">369.8&#x000b1;424.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">167.0&#x000b1;95.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.002</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Total levels fused</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.0&#x000b1;1.1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.5&#x000b1;0.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.8&#x000b1;1.0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Last follow-up (days)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">823.0&#x000b1;1125.9</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">892.9&#x000b1;1269.2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">723.6&#x000b1;890.4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.481</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Occipital condyle fracture</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (4.3)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 (5.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (2.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.640</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;C1 fracture</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (6.5)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (1.9)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 (13.2)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.078</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;C2 fracture</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9 (9.8)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 (5.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (15.8)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.154</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">LIV</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;C2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">36 (39.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">34 (63.0)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (5.3)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;C3</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">30 (32.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15 (27.8)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15 (39.5)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;C4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15 (16.3)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 (5.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 (31.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;C5</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9 (9.8)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (3.7)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7 (18.4)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;&#x000a0;C6</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (2.2)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (5.3)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec><sec><title>Perioperative variables</title><p>Patients with C1 instrumented had a longer operative time (192.7 &#x000b1; 68.8 vs. 166.3 &#x000b1; 40.5 min, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.032) and larger intraoperative blood loss (369.8 &#x000b1; 424.8 vs. 167.0 &#x000b1; 95.8, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.002). However, instrumenting C1 was associated with decreased cervical levels fused (2.5 &#x000b1; 0.8 vs. 3.8 &#x000b1; 1.0, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001). The most common lowest instrumented vertebra in patients with C1 instrumentation was C2 (63.0%), compared to C3 (39.5%) in patients without C1 instrumentation (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001) [<xref rid="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-T1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>].</p></sec><sec><title>Postoperative outcomes</title><p>Patients with C1 instrumentation had a higher rate of postoperative SSIs (11.1% vs. 0.0%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.040). There was no difference in overall mechanical complications (1.9% vs. 2.6%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 1.000), reoperation (5.6% vs. 7.9%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 0.688), or reoperation for mechanical complications (1.9% vs. 0.0%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> = 1.000) [<xref rid="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>]. The most common mechanical complications were screw loosening (<italic toggle="yes">N</italic> = 1, 50.0%), instrumentation failure (<italic toggle="yes">N</italic> = 1, 50.0%), and pseudarthrosis (<italic toggle="yes">N</italic> = 1, 50.0%) [<xref rid="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>].</p><table-wrap position="float" id="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-T2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Postoperative outcomes</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1"><thead valign="top"><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Characteristic</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Total (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=92), <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> (%)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">With C1 screw (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=54), <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> (%)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Without C1 screw (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=38), <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> (%)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>
</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Postoperative infection</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (6.5)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (11.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.040</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mechanical complications</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (2.2)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (1.9)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (2.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.000</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Screw loosening</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (1.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (2.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.413</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rod fracture</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (1.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (2.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.413</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pseudarthrosis</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (1.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (1.9)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.000</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reoperation</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (6.5)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 (5.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 (7.9)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.688</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reoperation for mechanical complications</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (1.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (1.9)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.000</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec><sec><title>Multivariable regression</title><p>Regarding our primary outcome of levels fused, after multivariable linear regression controlling for age, sex, and BMI, C1 instrumentation was independently associated with decreased levels fused (<italic toggle="yes">&#x003b2;</italic> = &#x02212;1.06, 95% confidence interval = -1.46 &#x02013; -0.67, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001). Conversely, this means that skipping C1 was independently associated with 1.1 more levels fused [<xref rid="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-T3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>].</p><table-wrap position="float" id="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-T3"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Univariate and multivariable regression controlling for age, sex, and body mass index</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1"><thead valign="top"><tr><th align="left" rowspan="2" colspan="1">Outcome</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" colspan="1">Risk factor</th><th align="center" colspan="2" rowspan="1">Univariate<hr/></th><th align="center" colspan="2" rowspan="1">Multivariable<hr/></th></tr><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x003b2; (95% CI)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>
</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x003b2; (95% CI)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>
</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Total fused levels</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">C1 Instrumentation</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;1.29 (&#x02212;1.66&#x02013;&#x02212;0.92)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;1.06 (&#x02212;1.46&#x02013;&#x02212;0.67)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="TF1-1"><p>CI: Confidence interval</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec><title>Representative cases</title><p>A 21-year-old male presented to the Emergency Room with a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 7 and purposeful movement in bilateral upper extremities. Cervical X-ray at admission showed 3 mm AOD and C1-C2 widening, along with bilateral avulsion fractures of the medial aspect of the occipital condyle. The patient underwent O-C2 fusion with occipital screws, C2 lateral mass crews, and C2 pars screws placed bilaterally. Postoperatively, he had improved strength in upper and lower extremities without any infections or mechanical complications [<xref rid="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-F1" ref-type="fig">Figure 1</xref>].</p><fig position="float" id="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>A 21-year-old male presented to the Emergency Room in an unresponsive state after a motor vehicle collision. The patient had a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 7 with purposeful movement in his upper extremities. Cervical X-ray at admission showed atlantooccipital dissociation, C1-C2 widening, and T1-T2 anterolisthesis with a fracture of the superior and inferior articular processes at T2 (a and b). The patient underwent O-C2 fusion and C7-T3 fusion. For the occipitocervical fusion, occipital screws, C2 lateral mass crews, and C2 pars screws were placed bilaterally (c-e). Postoperatively, he had improved strength in upper and lower extremities without any infections or mechanical complications</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCVJS-16-458-g001" position="float"/></fig><p>A 44-year-old female presented to the hospital after a motor vehicle collision with triple flexion responses in the lower extremities, flexure posturing in the right upper extremity, and minimal withdrawal in the right upper extremity. Preoperative X-ray showed severe atlantoaxial and AO dissociation. The patient underwent O-C5 fusion with open reduction of the atlantoaxial and AO subluxation. A midline occipital plate was placed with lateral mass screws at C3, C4, C5, and C6. The poor bone quality at C4 and C5 necessitated extension of the fusion to C6. At 3 years postoperatively, she presented with neck pain along with numbness and weakness. Imaging revealed a fracture of the occipital plate and loosening of the left C6 screw. Despite mechanical failure with neurological deficits, the patient did not undergo a revision surgery due to personal preference [<xref rid="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-F2" ref-type="fig">Figure 2</xref>].</p><fig position="float" id="jcvjs.jcvjs_155_25-F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>A 44-year-old female presented to the hospital after a motor vehicle collision with triple flexion responses in the lower extremity, flexure posturing in the right upper extremity, and minimal withdrawal in the right upper extremity. Preoperative X-ray (a and b) showed severe atlantoaxial and atlantooccipital dissociation. The patient was taken to the operating room and underwent O-C5 fusion, which was extended to C6 with open reduction of the atlantoaxial and atlantooccipital subluxation. A midline occipital plate was placed with lateral mass screws at C3, C4, C5, and C6 (c and d). Postoperatively, at 3 years, she presented with neck pain along with numbness and weakness. Imaging revealed a fracture of the occipital plate and loosening of the left C6 screw (e and f). The patient did not undergo a revision surgery due to personal preference</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCVJS-16-458-g002" position="float"/></fig></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion"><title>DISCUSSION</title><p>In OC fusions, we evaluated the impact of C1 screw placement on construct length and postoperative outcomes. Regarding our primary outcome, skipping C1 led to significantly more levels fused, which is understandable given the need for additional caudal fixation. That said, instrumenting C1 was significantly associated with greater blood loss, operative time, and SSI. There was no association between C1 instrumentation and mechanical complications, including instrumentation failure, screw loosening, or pseudarthrosis.</p><p>The current study demonstrated that skipping the C1 screw led to longer overall constructs. Skipping the C1 screw can cause a relative reduction in the biomechanical stability, necessitating additional levels of fixation, which results in more distal levels instrumented.[<xref rid="R27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>] To compensate for the loss of a crucial anchor point at C1, constructs usually need to be extended to allow for even distribution of load and assist in decreasing excessive motion at the C1-C2 junction.[<xref rid="R14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref><xref rid="R27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref><xref rid="R28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>] Most of our constructs that skipped C1 had a LIV of C3 (40%), C4 (32%), C5 (18%), or C6 (5%). Secondary to the moment-arm phenomena, a longer construct inherently increases the stress on adjacent segments, which may predispose those levels to accelerated degeneration[<xref rid="R29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>] and increase the risk of mechanical complications.[<xref rid="R14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>] Moreover, as the construct extends farther into the subaxial levels, the cervical levels most common to develop degeneration and stenosis are further at risk where the canal is narrower, such as C4-C5, C5-C6, and C6-C7.[<xref rid="R30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref><xref rid="R31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>] Zileli and Ak&#x00131;nt&#x000fc;rk[<xref rid="R32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>] evaluated postoperative complication rates after OC fusions and found that 87% of patients who required implant removal had constructs extending below C3. The implant removal was secondary to infection not controlled by medications, local pain, dissatisfaction with restriction of head movements, swallowing or respiratory issues, and screw fracture.[<xref rid="R32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>] Similarly, Mummaneni <italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>.[<xref rid="R33" ref-type="bibr">33</xref>] assessed patients with C1 lateral mass fixation and found that longer cervical constructs were associated with higher rates of complications, including pseudarthrosis.</p><p>Operative time and infection risk are important considerations, and instrumenting C1 led to an increase in operative time and blood loss, predisposing the patients to an increased risk of infections. The prolonged operative time in patients with C1 instrumentation can be explained by the extensive surgical dissection, venous plexus surrounding the C1 lateral mass, and the inherent added time in the placement of two additional high-risk screws.[<xref rid="R14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref><xref rid="R24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref><xref rid="R25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>] Prior studies,[<xref rid="R34" ref-type="bibr">34</xref><xref rid="R35" ref-type="bibr">35</xref><xref rid="R36" ref-type="bibr">36</xref>] including Gerometta <italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>.[<xref rid="R37" ref-type="bibr">37</xref>] have shown that both longer operative times and longer overall construct length are significant risk factors for the development of postoperative infection.[<xref rid="R37" ref-type="bibr">37</xref>] Some studies suggest that adequate C2 pedicle screw fixation may preclude the C1 screw in standard cases.[<xref rid="R15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>] The C2 pedicle screw can provide sufficient biomechanical stability and support for the cervical spine, allowing an eventual effective fusion and preventing the need for additional fixation at C1.[<xref rid="R20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref><xref rid="R22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>]</p><p>C1 instrumentation carries inherent risks and benefits, necessitating individualized surgical decision-making based on patient-specific factors such as age, comorbidities, BMI, bone quality, and severity of injury.[<xref rid="R38" ref-type="bibr">38</xref><xref rid="R39" ref-type="bibr">39</xref><xref rid="R40" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>] Although C1 fixation can often be avoided due to increased operative time and risk of complications, it becomes indispensable when there is marked instability at the C1 level.[<xref rid="R16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>] Our findings suggest that in most patients, omitting C1 instrumentation achieves adequate biomechanical stability while minimizing perioperative risk &#x02013; particularly operative time, blood loss, and postoperative infection.[<xref rid="R16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref><xref rid="R18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>] These results directly inform clinical practice: When C1 fixation is technically feasible and biomechanically warranted, it may allow for shorter constructs and fewer distal fixation points, potentially reducing the burden on adjacent segments, especially something to consider in younger patients with many decades of life left. Conversely, in medically complex or osteoporotic patients, skipping C1 may be more appropriate, provided that extended fixation is used to compensate for the loss of proximal fixation points.[<xref rid="R41" ref-type="bibr">41</xref>] Thus, preoperative planning should prioritize a tailored approach, balancing anatomical constraints, bone quality, and the biomechanical demands of each case to optimize both short- and long-term outcomes. Ultimately, our study highlights that the choice to skip or include C1 screws is not merely technical but a strategic decision that can significantly impact construct length, complication rates, and surgical morbidity.</p><p>The current study has several limitations that must be addressed. First, as a retrospective cohort study, it is subject to selection bias and confounding variables that may not have been fully accounted for despite controlling in multivariable analysis. Second, the heterogeneity in surgical techniques, surgeon preferences, and instrumentation choices over the nearly two-decade study period could influence outcomes. Third, the relatively short follow-up limits the ability to assess long-term outcomes, including mechanical complications, improvement in pain, and reoperation rates. Fourth, the small sample size may limit the statistical power to detect meaningful differences in outcomes. Finally, patient-reported outcomes, including functional status and quality of life measures, were not comprehensively analyzed, limiting our understanding of the clinical impact of skipping C1 screws beyond mechanical and surgical outcomes. Future prospective studies with longer follow-up and standardized assessment protocols are needed to better define the risks and benefits of including C1 screws in OC fusion for traumatic cervical injury.</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusion"><title>CONCLUSION</title><p>In patients undergoing OC fusion for cervical trauma, 41% did not have C1 screws. Skipping C1 screws was associated with longer fusions with lesser operative time, decreased blood loss, and decreased infection, with no association with mechanical complications or reoperation. These mixed results highlight the pros and cons of skipping C1 screws in OC fusion for traumatic cervical spine injury.</p><sec sec-type="COI-statement"><title>Conflicts of interest</title><p>Dr. Zuckerman reports being an unaffiliated neurotrauma consultant for the National Football League and a consultant for Medtronic. Dr. Stephens receives institutional research funding from Globus, has received institutional research funding from Stryker, and is an editorial board member for the research journal Spine Research. Dr. Abtahi received an institutional research support from Stryker Spine. No other perceived conflict of interest by any of the listed authors.</p></sec></sec></body><back><ref-list><title>REFERENCES</title><ref id="R1"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="journal" publication-format="print"><person-group person-group-type="author">
